  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="rail">
        <dl>
          <dt>Advisor</dt><dd>Ali Jannesari</dd>
          <dt>Department</dt><dd>Computer Science</dd>
          <dt>Status</dt><dd>Ongoing</dd>
        </dl>
        <hr class="rail-rule" />
        <dl>
          <dt>Bears on</dt>
          <dd>LLM serving · metastable failures · admission control</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div>
        <article class="entry">
          <h2 class="entry-title">Metastable failures in prefix-cached LLM agent serving</h2>
          <p class="entry-meta">Ali Jannesari · SwaPP Lab, Computer Science, Iowa State University · ongoing</p>
          <p>
            An agent works in a loop: model call, tool call, model call, re-sending the
            whole growing conversation each turn. Serving engines such as SGLang and
            vLLM keep that shared prefix in a GPU KV cache so a follow-up turn costs a
            fraction of the first. The cache is small, though. When enough agent
            sessions compete for it, a session evicted while it waits on a tool has to
            recompute everything on its next turn, and that extra work keeps the cache
            full and evicts the next session. The question is whether a short burst of
            traffic can push an otherwise healthy server into a self-sustaining
            all-miss, low-throughput state, a metastable failure, and which scheduling
            and admission mechanisms prevent it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Three instruments. A discrete-event simulator of a continuous-batching
            engine with prefix caching, covering scheduling policies, eviction rules,
            memory-allocation modes and re-implementations of recently proposed
            mechanisms. A queueing-theoretic thrashing-curve model that connects the
            classical working-set and metastability literature to agent workloads and
            says when caching helps, when it collapses, and when admission control
            restores stability. And a load-testing harness that drives a production
            engine, SGLang serving a 7B-parameter model on NVIDIA A100s, on Iowa
            State's Nova cluster through SLURM, with the prediction for each run
            committed before the run and paired control arms under common random
            numbers. On the real engine, cache reuse collapses sharply once concurrent
            sessions exceed the cache's working set and throughput falls severalfold,
            which matches the simulator. Whether the collapse outlives the burst that
            caused it is the experiment in progress.
          </p>
          <p class="entry-meta">
            Public overview:
            <a href="https://github.com/AkshatValse/Nova-Research-Overview">Nova-Research-Overview</a>.
            Code and data held back pending the preprint.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

