  <section class="section shell">
    <div class="row">
      <div class="rail">
        <dl>
          <dt>Advisor</dt><dd>Hongyang Gao</dd>
          <dt>Department</dt><dd>Computer Science</dd>
          <dt>Status</dt><dd>Ongoing</dd>
        </dl>
        <hr class="rail-rule" />
        <dl>
          <dt>Bears on</dt>
          <dd>conformal prediction · delayed feedback · volatility forecasting</dd>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <div>
        <article class="entry">
          <h2 class="entry-title">Online calibration of neural volatility forecasts with early, one-sided feedback</h2>
          <p class="entry-meta">Hongyang Gao · Computer Science, Iowa State University · ongoing</p>
          <p>
            A forecast of a cumulative quantity, the realized variance of an asset over
            the next day, say, is scored only when the horizon ends. Every online
            calibration method therefore learns one full horizon late, which is exactly
            when it is slowest to react to a shift. But the increments of such a
            quantity are non-negative, so the running sum is monotone, and if it crosses
            the issued upper bound before the horizon ends the miss is certain at once.
            Coverage is confirmed only at the end. The feedback is early, but
            one-sided.
          </p>
          <p>
            The method is a model-agnostic layer on top of any quantile forecaster. It
            credits certain early misses to an online integrator as soon as they are
            confirmed, keeps explicit accounts of forecasts still pending so the
            eventual miss count is known to lie in an interval rather than treated as
            settled, and applies a bounded correction only when the accounting
            justifies it. The long-run coverage guarantee under early crediting, bounded
            correction and missing data is proved and checked numerically over tens of
            thousands of adversarial runs. Whether this recovers faster after a
            volatility jump than delayed-feedback conformal baselines, without widening
            the bounds the rest of the time, is the question, and the answer is being
            settled on a regime-shift simulation with exact oracle quantiles and on a
            sealed real-data test set, against pre-registered hypotheses.
          </p>
          <p class="entry-meta">
            Public overview:
            <a href="https://github.com/AkshatValse/neural-forecasting-research-overview">neural-forecasting-research-overview</a>.
            Code, proofs and results held back pending submission.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

