      <div>
        <article class="entry">
          <h2 class="entry-title">Reliability of tool-using agents</h2>
          <p class="entry-meta">Yang Li · Computer Science, Iowa State University · ongoing</p>
          <p>
            Two questions about agents that run for a long time and sometimes fail.
            When one stalls, when is restarting it worth more than waiting, and what in
            the distribution of run times decides that? And how do you estimate the
            probability of a failure too rare for direct simulation to reach, the same
            problem as the rare-event work above with an agent in place of a queue? Two
            pilots on real agents, one on restart policies and one on rare failures,
            each with its kill criteria registered before any data, with the direction
            still being chosen between them.
          </p>
          <p class="entry-meta">
            Approach and results held back pending the choice of direction.
          </p>
        </article>
